{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"sugarcane-scrummy-arctichare","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Summary\n\n`rebar fsck` has TWO different issue counts and they disagree.\n\n- **The exit code** comes from `_scan`'s counter, which respects each finding's `is_issue` flag:\n  `src/rebar/_commands/fsck.py:242-243` (`rc = 0 if issue_count == 0 else 1`), fed by\n  `src/rebar/_commands/fsck_tracker_health.py:58-70`.\n- **`--output json`'s `issue_count`** is recomputed independently as `len(issues)` at\n  `src/rebar/_commands/fsck.py:139`, where `issues` is EVERY uppercase `KIND:` line the text\n  report emitted (`fsck.py:120-138`) — with no knowledge of `is_issue`.\n\nSo the never-counted kinds inflate the JSON count while contributing nothing to the exit code.\n\n## Affected kinds (all pre-existing)\n\n- `PUSH_PENDING` — `fsck_tracker_health.py:232-238` (`is_issue=False`)\n- `TRACKER_DIRTY_TMP_EVENT` — `fsck_tracker_health.py:276-281` (`is_issue=False`)\n- `WARN:` lines — index-lock (`fsck_scan.py:157-161`), forward-compat (`fsck_scan.py:244-249`)\n- `STATUS_FORK_RESOLVED` — joins this set as of bug `326f-595a-088b-40a5`\n\nThe divergence is already observable and test-pinned today:\n`tests/unit/test_doctor_dirty_tree.py:202-218` asserts `rc == 1` while\n`tracker_dirty_tmp_event` sits inside `payload[\"issues\"]`, and\n`tests/unit/test_unsigned_env_fsck_heldout.py:118-128` documents that JSON\n`issue_count == len(issues)` literally.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nA JSON consumer that gates on `issue_count > 0` reaches a different verdict than a shell\nconsumer that gates on the exit code, against the same store and the same run. `docs/user-guide.md:449-450`\ntells users `TRACKER_DIRTY_TMP_EVENT` is \"never counted\" — which is true of the exit code and\nfalse of the JSON payload they were pointed at.\n\n## Discovered from\n\nBug `326f-595a-088b-40a5` (cutprice-untameable-stork), while making the MCP fsck tool return a\nstructured result. Deliberately NOT fixed there: reconciling the two counts changes CLI JSON\noutput for kinds that bug does not own, so it needs its own decision (does `issue_count` mean\n\"lines emitted\" or \"things that failed the run\"? — likely: keep `issues[]` complete, make\n`issue_count` the counted subset, and consider an explicit per-issue `counted` boolean).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Decide the meaning of `--output json`'s `issue_count` and make it agree with the exit code.\n- [ ] `issues[]` keeps carrying every emitted finding, counted or not (no loss of information).\n- [ ] Every never-counted kind above behaves per the decision, with a test pinning each.\n- [ ] `docs/user-guide.md`'s \"never counted\" wording is true of both surfaces.","id":"29c3-b025-04d7-454e","parent_id":"","priority":3,"tags":["fsck","cli","json","consistency"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"fsck --output json issue_count disagrees with the exit code for never-counted finding kinds"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787859313192922852,"uuid":"0a671662-b67c-4696-9faa-c179c9c17591"}